<h2>Connecting an agent</h2>
<p>
    Point an MCP-aware client (Claude Code configured with a remote MCP server) at this host's
    <code>/mcp</code> endpoint with an <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header carrying
    the service's bearer token; tool discovery, calling, and result parsing are handled by the client
    library itself. The server exposes twenty tools:
</p>
<pre class="run-output">dotnet_run                        - start a `dotnet` command against a repository
git_run                           - start a `git` command against a repository
read_run_output                   - poll a run started by dotnet_run/git_run/clone_repository for
                                     incremental output and its eventual exit code
cancel_run                        - cancel a run that is still in flight
list_repositories                 - list the repositories available on this host
clone_repository                  - clone a new repository onto this host
delete_repository                 - permanently delete a repository and everything inside it
find_files                        - search a repository for files/directories by glob pattern (*, **,
                                     ?), preferred over `git_run` for locating files
get_file_info                     - get a filesystem entry's type, size, attributes, owner, dates, and
                                     lock status, preferred over `git_run` for file metadata questions
delete_path                       - permanently delete one file or directory inside a repository
authorize_git_host                - store a personal access token (PAT) for a remote git host, so
                                     git_run/clone_repository can authenticate against it
list_authorized_git_hosts         - list every host with a stored credential, its authorization date,
                                     and whether it currently appears to be working
revoke_git_host_authorization     - remove a host's stored credential
authorize_nuget_feed              - store a personal access token (PAT) for a NuGet feed, so dotnet_run
                                     can authenticate against it during restore
list_authorized_nuget_feeds       - list every NuGet feed with a stored credential and its authorization date
revoke_nuget_feed_authorization   - remove a NuGet feed's stored credential
transfer_file                     - read a file's contents (base64-encoded) from within a named repository
get_run_resources                 - poll a run's recent CPU/RAM trend, to judge whether it's hung or still working
get_environment_info              - inspect this host's installed dotnet/git toolchain, SDKs, workloads, NuGet
                                     sources, and disk space
get_file_lock_info                - find which process(es) have a file open, to diagnose a Windows "file in use"
                                     build/test failure</pre>
<p>
    <code>dotnet_run</code>/<code>git_run</code>/<code>clone_repository</code> return a run id
    immediately rather than blocking until the command finishes - poll <code>read_run_output</code>
    with that run id for incremental output. Every other tool, including all three deletion tools, is
    synchronous. <code>transfer_file</code>, <code>get_file_lock_info</code>, <code>find_files</code>,
    <code>get_file_info</code>, and <code>delete_path</code> are all confined to the named repository's
    own directory - none of them can read, inspect, or delete anything outside it - and
    <code>transfer_file</code> is rejected outright if the file exceeds the configured size limit.
    <code>delete_repository</code> and <code>delete_path</code> are irreversible: there is no undo, no
    recycle bin, and no confirmation step - every delete is recorded in this dashboard's run history so
    what was removed and when is always auditable after the fact, but the file itself is gone.
    A personal access token passed to <code>authorize_git_host</code> is written once into this
    host's own git credential store and never read back - it is never logged, never included in any
    tool result, and never appears in a process command line. A <code>git_run</code>/
    <code>clone_repository</code> failure that looks authentication-related is called out
    specifically, naming <code>authorize_git_host</code> as the fix, rather than only relaying git's
    raw error text. A personal access token passed to <code>authorize_nuget_feed</code> is stored
    depending on the feed: an Azure DevOps Artifacts feed's token is encrypted and held by this
    service for the Azure Artifacts Credential Provider to use; any other feed's token is written
    once into this machine's own NuGet configuration, encrypted by NuGet's own mechanism. Neither is
    ever logged, included in any tool result, or passed as a process command-line argument.
</p>

<h2>Trusting the certificate</h2>
<p>
    This service's HTTPS certificate is self-signed - private by design, not issued by a publicly
    trusted certificate authority - so both a browser and the sbx sandbox's MCP client need to be
    told to trust it explicitly, or every connection will show a warning (browser) or fail TLS
    verification outright (a typical MCP client).
</p>
@if (_certificateAvailable)
{
    <p>
        <a href="/dashboard-certificate" download="outofthebox.cer">Download the certificate (outofthebox.cer)</a>
        - a public-only file with no private key, safe to copy anywhere it's needed.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <p class="empty-state">
        No certificate file is configured on this instance (typical for a bare <code>dotnet run</code>
        without one set up) - see <code>INSTALL.md</code>'s "Certificate" section for how to generate
        one, including the public-only file this download needs.
    </p>
}

<h3>On a Windows PC, for the dashboard</h3>
<p>
    Double-click the downloaded <code>outofthebox.cer</code> file, select
    <strong>Install Certificate...</strong>, choose <strong>Current User</strong> (just for your own
    account) or <strong>Local Machine</strong> (every account on that PC - prompts for
    administrator elevation), then <strong>Place all certificates in the following store</strong>
    and browse to <strong>Trusted Root Certification Authorities</strong>. Confirm through the
    security warning (self-signed certificates always show one) - your browser stops warning about
    this host from then on.
</p>
<p>Equivalently, from an elevated PowerShell prompt:</p>
<pre class="run-output">Import-Certificate -FilePath outofthebox.cer -CertStoreLocation Cert:\LocalMachine\Root</pre>

<h3>In the sbx sandbox, for the MCP connection</h3>
<p>
    Copy the downloaded certificate onto the sandbox, then use whichever of these matches how the
    sandbox actually makes its outbound connection:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>System-wide trust (most Linux distributions)</strong>: copy it to
        <code>/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/outofthebox.crt</code> (the <code>.crt</code>
        extension matters here) and run <code>sudo update-ca-certificates</code> - every tool that
        reads the system trust store (<code>curl</code>, <code>openssl</code>, most language
        runtimes) picks it up automatically from then on, no further per-tool configuration needed.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Node.js</strong> (the runtime Claude Code itself runs on): set
        <code>NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/outofthebox.cer</code> in the environment the sandbox's
        Claude Code process runs in - Node's own TLS stack reads this directly, without touching the
        OS trust store at all.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><code>curl</code>, for a manual connectivity check</strong>: pass
        <code>--cacert /path/to/outofthebox.cer</code> explicitly, e.g.
        <code>curl --cacert outofthebox.cer https://&lt;host&gt;:&lt;port&gt;/mcp</code>.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    Exactly how you register a remote MCP server (and thus where to point it at this certificate)
    depends on your sandbox's own Claude Code configuration mechanism - nothing repository-specific
    to configure beyond trusting this one file.
</p>

@code {
    private bool _certificateAvailable;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        _certificateAvailable = !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Options.Value.CertificateFilePath) && File.Exists(Options.Value.CertificateFilePath);
}
